  "overview": {
    "text": "Muhammad (c. 570–632) was an Arabian religious and community leader whose preaching in Mecca in the early seventh century became the beginning of Islam. Muslims believe God revealed the Qur’an to him through the angel Gabriel; in Islamic belief, he is the final prophet in a line that includes Abraham, Moses, and Jesus. His message called people to worship one God. In 622, he and his followers migrated from Mecca to Medina in the hijra, where he helped establish a Muslim community; the migration marks the start of the Islamic calendar. Muslims also look to his reported words and actions, preserved in hadith and sunnah, as guidance alongside the Qur’an. Historical details in later biographies are not all equally certain.",
